"""Writes index/revenue-household-incidence-model.md from incidence.py and years.py (v1.4)."""
from incidence import ROWS, OPTIONS, K, SCEN, run, gf_test, per_hh, INC, GROUPS
from years import series, YRS
m = lambda v: ("+" if v >= 0 else "−") + f"${abs(v):,.0f}"
res = {n: run(o, dr) for n, (o, dr) in SCEN.items()}
gfs = {n: gf_test(o) for n, (o, dr) in SCEN.items()}
B = "B. Base plus the full options list (gap about $0.2T)"
G = "G. EDU-9B education fund as the closer in place of C19 and C20"
rb, lines = res[B]; rg = res[G][0]
q4 = lambda n: res[n][0]["Q4"]["net"]
yb = series(["C18", "C19", "C20", "C5"]); yd = series(["C18", "C19b", "C20", "C5"]); yg = series(["C18", "C5", "EDUP", "EDUB", "C35"])
o = []; A = o.append
A("# Revenue: D3 household net test, incidence model v1.4\n")
A("Oct 20, 2026 · Espen Swanson · Generated from index/2029-trifecta-master-ledger.md (education batch on top of the full rebuild: base $6.37T, gap $4.13T, about −$0.05T after every option) by index/tools/incidence-model.py, incidence-years.py and incidence-make-md.py. Role: index. Regenerate it rather than editing by hand.\n")
A("This is a first pass at the test Dec. D3 sets: every income group below the top comes out ahead on net from the agenda's combined taxes, fees and benefits, and new general-fund revenue comes mostly from the top 1% and corporations. The unit is the bottom four quintiles, with the top quintile split out. Figures are average dollars per household per year over 2030-39 (nominal), from ten-year totals divided across about 140M households (estimate). Every distribution share is an **estimate** anchored to a published pattern; no row is yet a microsimulation.\n")
A("**Planning basis (Decision Brief 1, Oct 20): scenario P.** Package B (C18, C35, C19b, C20) plus the top-end sprint package C38 (REV-TS1 to TS11, about $0.89T, mostly corporate and top-1% items) is now the planning basis; the program trims are not yet decided and are left out. Every quintile below the top comes out ahead: Q1 +$10,952, Q2 +$9,429, Q3 +$5,867 and **Q4 +$1,304** a household a year on the ten-year average, and 72% of the new general-fund revenue comes from the top 1% and corporations. Q4 is still behind in 2030 (about −$1,509), roughly even in 2031 (+$380) and +$1,800 by 2034. The 2030 dip is the remaining D3 exposure; its levers are unchanged (start C19b with the public option in 2031, or front-load the dividend).\n")
A("**What changed in v1.4 (later on Oct 20, Espen's decisions).** The carbon fee moves to the durability session's price path ($25 a ton in 2030 rising to about $100 by 2037, road fuels still exempt): the fee is $1.35T, the Climate Dividend $1.08T and the low-income share of the transition money $0.108T over ten years, and all three now follow the fee's own year profile (low early, rising), so the fee's bite in 2030-31 is smaller than on the old path, and so is the early dividend. C35 (the estate tax) is restated as the ~$0.15T increment beyond G9. **Effects:** on scenario B the fourth quintile's ten-year average moves from +$779 to +$756 (the fee grows by $0.10T and the dividend plus low-income share by $0.09T; every quintile still comes out ahead). In 2030 the fourth quintile improves by about $100 (−$1,926 to −$1,823), and 2031 turns slightly positive; the first quintile gives up about $90 in 2030 because the early dividend is smaller too. Front-loading the dividend (paying the 2030 dividend at the 2031 rate from a Treasury advance repaid from later receipts) is the lever if that matters.\n")
A("**What changed in v1.3 (Oct 20, education batch).** Education moved to its own domain file (domains/domain-education.md):\n- **FAM-4 became EDU-1 and EDU-5** at the same $0.65T, so the old F4 row is now `EDU`.\n- **EDU-3 universal school meals** ($0.27T) was added as a benefit on a new `meals` key (the increment over today's free and reduced-price meals lands mostly on the second to fourth quintiles).\n- **EDU-4 educator pipeline** ($0.03T) was added as a benefit, and **EDU-7** (repealing the scholarship tax credit) as a $0.04T cost on donors.\n- **New options:** C35 (the estate tax at 2009 parameters), C36 (meals at community-eligibility scale) and the EDU-9B education fund (an uncapped payroll contribution of about 1.4 points plus the new benefits it would add). **Scenario G** tests EDU-9B as the closer in place of C19 and C20; **scenario H** adds C35 to B.\n\nv1.2's changes (housing, the public-safety package, LAB-8 and LAB-9) are carried forward.\n")
A("## Bottom line\n")
A(f"- **On the full options list (B), the bottom four quintiles all come out ahead; the fourth by about {m(q4(B))} a household a year** ({rb['Q4']['pct']}% of income), up from +$546 in v1.2, mostly because universal meals reach it. The first three gain clearly: {m(rb['Q1']['net'])}, {m(rb['Q2']['net'])} and {m(rb['Q3']['net'])}.")
A(f"- **The employer health-exclusion cap (C19) is still the swing.** Without it the fourth quintile gains about {m(q4('C. B without the employer-exclusion cap (C19)'))}; with the cap at the 75th percentile, about {m(q4('D. B with the exclusion cap at the 75th percentile (C19b)'))}. Drop the hospital-price pass-through to wages and the full list lands at {m(q4('E. B with no premium pass-through from the price caps'))} for the fourth quintile, which is a coin-flip.")
A(f"- **The education fund (EDU-9B) passes the quintile test as a closer, but it is not free for the fourth quintile.** Replacing C19 and C20 with EDU-9B's contribution (about 1.4 points, all counted on workers) and its added benefits leaves the first three quintiles better off than B ({m(rg['Q1']['net'])}, {m(rg['Q2']['net'])}, {m(rg['Q3']['net'])}) and the fourth slightly worse ({m(rg['Q4']['net'])} against {m(q4(B))}). It is roughly gap-neutral against B: it relieves the general fund of about $2.0T (claims moved into the fund plus current spending it absorbs) against the $1.92T of C19 and C20 it replaces. Whether to use a middle-reaching contribution at all is a D3 and D4 decision, not a modeling result.")
A(f"- **The revenue-mix test passes** in every scenario: the top 1% (by incidence) and corporations (statutory) bear about {gfs[B][3]}% of new general-fund revenue on the full list and {gfs['A. Base package only (gap unfunded)'][3]}% on the base alone; the rule is more than half. EDU-9B's contribution is a dedicated fund and is not counted as general-fund revenue.")
A(f"- **The estate tax (C35) is invisible below the top:** scenario H moves no quintile below the top and takes about ${(res['H. B plus the estate tax (C35)'][0]['T1']['net'] - rb['T1']['net'])*-1:,.0f} more a year from the top 1%.")
A(f"- **The 80th-99th percentiles lose about {m(-rb['Q5x']['net']).replace('+','')} a year** on the full list, where C18 and C20 land.")
A("- **Averages hide losers inside the fourth quintile.** A household there without children, without marketplace or Medicare coverage, and with employer coverage above the median premium gets few of the benefits and most of the costs; under EDU-9B the same household also pays the contribution and gets no school benefit. The next version needs within-quintile spread.\n")
A("## Results by scenario\n")
A("Net = benefits minus costs, dollars per household per year, 2030-39 average.\n")
A("| Scenario | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | 80-99th | Top 1% | Top 1% + corporations, share of new GF revenue | Passes? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for n in SCEN:
    r = res[n][0]; ok = all(r[g]["net"] > 0 for g in GROUPS[:4])
    note = "Yes" if ok else "No (Q4)"
    if n.startswith("A."): note += " (but the gap is unfunded)"
    A(f"| {n} | {m(r['Q1']['net'])} | {m(r['Q2']['net'])} | {m(r['Q3']['net'])} | {m(r['Q4']['net'])} | {m(r['Q5']['net'])} | {m(r['Q5x']['net'])} | {m(r['T1']['net'])} | {gfs[n][3]}% | {note} |")
A("\nScenario B adds the options that reach households: C18, C19 (median), C20 and C5. The other options (C17, C21, C29-C34, C36) do not reach households directly through a tax. Scenario G swaps C19 and C20 for EDU-9B (its contribution EDUP, its added benefits EDUB, and the estate tax dedicated to the fund). Scenario A fails D4 (the gap is unfunded) and is shown for comparison only.\n")
A("### As a share of income (scenario B)\n\n| | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Top 1% |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
A("| Benefits | " + " | ".join(f"${rb[g]['benefit']:,}" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("| Costs | " + " | ".join(f"${rb[g]['cost']:,}" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("| Net, % of income | " + " | ".join(f"{rb[g]['pct']:+.1f}%" for g in GROUPS + ["T1"]) + " |")
A("\nHealth benefits are valued at government cost, as CBO does, so the bottom quintile's gain is large relative to cash income. Most of it is coverage, not cash.\n")
A("## What moves the fourth quintile (scenario B)\n\n| Item | Benefit or cost | Per Q4 household a year |\n| --- | --- | --- |")
items = sorted(((abs(per_hh(d['Q4'], 'Q4')), rid, label, side, per_hh(d['Q4'], 'Q4')) for rid, label, side, amt, d, basis in lines), reverse=True)
for _, rid, label, side, v in items[:25]:
    A(f"| {rid}: {label} | {side} | ${abs(v):,.0f}{' (negative)' if v < 0 else ''} |")
A("")
A("## By year (v1.4)\n")
A("Each ten-year total is spread over 2030-39 by a phase-in profile: about 4% annual growth for every row, plus ramps (estimates). Child care reaches the full cap in 2032. The public option opens in 2031 and auto-enrolls in 2032. Medicare hearing and vision start in 2031 and dental in 2033 (removed in-window under C5). Paid-leave benefits start in 2031 while contributions start in 2030. Moving America's fuel steps phase in through 2038. National service ramps to its 2034 scale, and the public-safety receipts start in 2031. **New in v1.3:** universal meals start with the 2030-31 school year, the IDEA glide path and Pell ramp, and the educator pipeline is front-loaded. Net dollars per household, nominal.\n")
A("| Year | B: Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | D (cap at 75th pct): Q4 | G (EDU-9B closer): Q4 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for y in YRS:
    A(f"| {y} | {m(yb[y]['Q1'])} | {m(yb[y]['Q2'])} | {m(yb[y]['Q3'])} | **{m(yb[y]['Q4'])}** | {m(yd[y]['Q4'])} | {m(yg[y]['Q4'])} |")
A(f"\n- **The fourth quintile is behind in 2030 and about even in 2031** on scenario B ({m(yb[2030]['Q4'])} and {m(yb[2031]['Q4'])}), then positive from 2032 and near +$1,250-1,300 from 2034. Universal meals help from late 2030; the early-year shortfall is still the carbon fee, leave contributions and the exclusion cap arriving before the public option and full child care.")
A("- **Year five (2034) passes for every quintile in every scenario**, which is D3's interim test. The 2030 shortfall still lands just before the November 2030 midterm.")
A(f"- **The 75th-percentile cap (D) turns Q4 positive from 2031**, but 2030 is still about {m(yd[2030]['Q4'])}. **EDU-9B (G) is worse in 2030-31** ({m(yg[2030]['Q4'])}, {m(yg[2031]['Q4'])}) because the contribution starts at full rate while pre-K seats ramp; starting the contribution with the 2031 school year, or at a lower first-year rate, is the lever.")
A("- **Fixing 2030 is a sequencing choice, not a size choice.** The levers: start C19 and the carbon fee in 2031, front-load the Climate Dividend, or start leave contributions with benefits in 2031. Each costs about a year of revenue and needs a D4 check.\n")
A("## Implications for open choices\n\nThese are options with their effects, not edits to any canonical file.\n")
A("1. **C19, the exclusion cap.** At the median premium it decides the fourth quintile. The 75th-percentile version passes with more margin and reopens about $0.59T of the gap.")
A("2. **EDU-9B, the education fund.** As a closer it beats C19 and C20 for the bottom three quintiles and costs the fourth about $140 a year against B. It needs Espen's call under D3 (a middle-reaching contribution) and D4 (a new dedicated fund) before it can be chosen.")
A("3. **C35, the estate tax.** Pure top-end revenue; adding it lets one middle-reaching option go.")
A("4. **SAF-R2.** Dropping the alcohol excise helps every quintile a little and the fourth by about $60 a year. It moves about $0.07T onto the top-end base.")
A("5. **The Climate Dividend.** Shifting part of the 20% transition share into the dividend (CLI-2C) helps every quintile equally.")
A("6. **Before final:** the hospital-price pass-through (H5p) remains the assumption most able to flip the result; it needs a sourced estimate.\n")
A("## Dynamic effects and the D3 test (Oct 20)\n")
A("The ledger now carries a dynamic sensitivity table (not in any total): under CBO/JCT-style conventions macroeconomic feedback is about −$0.56T over 2030-39 (−$1.42T to +$0.28T), and about +$0.55T under Talarico-style assumptions (estimates). It does not change the quintile results above, for three reasons. **Most of it is revenue, not household money:** the largest row is lower investment and more avoidance by corporations and the top 1%, which lands on the top quintile and the gap, not on the bottom four. **The household-facing pieces roughly offset:** the care labor-supply gain (about +$0.22T) raises earnings mostly in the second to fourth quintiles, while the small labor-supply drag from coverage and the Child Tax Credit falls on the same groups through slightly lower hours, a choice the household makes rather than a cost imposed on it. **Wage effects of corporate taxes are already in:** the model puts 25% of corporate taxes on workers (the JCT/CBO convention), which is the channel a dynamic score would otherwise add. If a dynamic gap had to be closed with middle-reaching revenue (C19 or C20) rather than top-end options, that choice would move the quintiles and should be run as its own scenario.\n")
A("## Limits of v1.4\n\n- **Shares are estimates.** Each row uses a pattern key; replacing them with published TPC and CBO tables is v2. The new `meals`, `donors` and `estate` keys are estimates.\n- **Year profiles are shapes, not scored annual paths.**\n- **No behavior.** Coverage shifts, avoidance beyond the ledger's 15% haircuts, and macro effects are left out (the education session's maternal-employment effect, plausibly $25-45B a year of federal revenue at maturity, is not counted).\n- **Public goods are left out** on both sides: the agency rebuild, transit service, climate credits to firms, defense, education research (EDU-8) and the civic and information rows.\n- **Full pass-through** of carbon, border, transport and alcohol charges to households, which is conservative for the middle. EDU-9B's contribution is counted entirely on workers.\n- **Corporate taxes** use the JCT/CBO convention: 25% labor, 75% capital.\n- **Not modeled:** the minimum wage (LAB-4), the H-1B fee, COM-5's bank-fee limits (a household benefit), veterans health (NSC-10), and immigration's legalization effects.\n")
A("## Model inputs\n\n| ID | Instrument | Side | 2030-39 ($T) | Key | Basis |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for rid, label, side, amt, parts, basis in ROWS:
    A(f"| {rid} | {label} | {side} | {amt} | {', '.join(k for k, _ in parts)} | {basis} |")
for oid, (side, amt, parts, label) in OPTIONS.items():
    A(f"| {oid} | {label} | {side} | {amt} | {', '.join(k for k, _ in parts)} | ledger option |")
A("\n**Distribution keys** (percent of the total by quintile, Q1 to Q5, and the top 1%'s share of the total). All are estimates; `meals`, `donors` and `estate` are new in v1.3.\n\n| Key | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Q5 | Top 1% |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |")
for k, (sh, t1) in K.items(): A(f"| {k} | " + " | ".join(str(x) for x in sh) + f" | {t1} |")
A("\nOther assumptions (estimates): about 140M households mid-window; 2034 average incomes of $23K, $60K, $105K, $180K and $520K by quintile, and $4.3M for the top 1%; 30% of Medicare Advantage capture passes to enrollees as fewer extras; 30% of clean-energy credits go to households; $0.30T of Part B premiums; $0.50T of hospital-price savings passed to wages. EDU-9B: ~1.4 points of uncapped payroll at ~$135B a point in 2030 growing 4% ($2.27T in 2030-39); $0.63T of benefits beyond the chosen rows (pre-K and child care share $0.24T; teacher floor, PARA and mental-health staff, Title I and IDEA top-ups $0.39T), from domains/domain-education.md.\n")
A("## Sources\n\n- [CBO, The Distribution of Household Income, 2022](https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62300)\n- [CBO budget option, Reduce Tax Subsidies for Employment-Based Health Benefits](https://www.cbo.gov/budget-options/60953)\n- [Fremstad and Paul, PERI WP434](https://peri.umass.edu/images/publication/WP434.pdf)\n- [TPC TaxVox, Who benefits from health-care related tax expenditures?](https://taxpolicycenter.org/taxvox/who-benefits-health-care-related-tax-expenditures)\n- [Tax Foundation on JCT's corporate incidence convention](https://taxfoundation.org/jct-corporate-tax-falls-partly-labor/)\n- [CBO budget option 52284, alcohol at $16 per proof gallon (2016)](https://www.cbo.gov/budget-options/2016/52284)\n- sources/education-transforming-education-act-analysis-and-trust-fund.md: EDU-3's increment and EDU-9B's rates and spending paths\n- index/2029-trifecta-master-ledger.md and the domain files for every amount\n")
open("revenue-household-incidence-model.md", "w").write("\n".join(o)); print(len("\n".join(o)))
